 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: America&#x27;s &quot;launch crisis&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 While SpaceX basked in its historic Starship " catch " last weekend, a different space mission went awry the next day — with serious implications for U.S. national security.

 United Launch Alliance's heavy-lift Vulcan rocket had a mission failure Sunday just 10 minutes into its mission to bring 40 Amazon Leo satellites to space. Why it matters: The growing commercial space industry and future U.S. military missions depend on multiple reliable rocket providers.

 But only two companies have flown National Security Space Launch missions: ULA and SpaceX. The former just had a mission failure, and the latter may be winding down government launches. Blue Origin will eventually become a third provider once its New Glenn rocket, which exploded on the launchpad in May, meets the required number of certification flights. The company is targeting January to return to flight, according to an internal memo . "It is very concerning, especially when the three main Pentagon providers are SpaceX, ULA and Blue Origin," Emmerson Overell, a research analyst at the Foundation for Defense of Democracies, told me. "And Blue Origin's New Glenn heavy rocket has also been grounded." What they're saying: ULA said in a press statement that it's investigating the cause and "[w]e truly understand the impact this has on our customers."

 ULA said yesterday that "preliminary indications" are that Vulcan's low booster thrust was the result of inadequate fuel supply and not an issue with the engines itself. The jointly owned Boeing and Lockheed Martin business had to sideline Vulcan in February for national security missions as it investigated a booster problem. Threat level: SpaceX's Starship development and ULA's mission failure have wide-ranging repercussions for national security and the satellite business.

 SpaceX plans to scale down the use of the reusable, medium-lift Falcon 9 rocket that's been the workhorse of the space industry and the U.S. military's operations. The company is going all in on its super-heavy-lift Starship — which was seemingly built with Starlink satellites, not other military or commercial payloads, in mind. ULA's Vulcan failure hurts not only Space Force but also Amazon, which partially relies on the rockets to launch satellites for its emerging space-based internet service. "Even once they get back to flight, how long is it going to take for them to get certified again to launch these sensitive national security payloads?" Todd Harrison, the American Enterprise Institute's space expert, told me. "This is a real mess." Brig. Gen. Eric Zarybnisky, the Space Force officer who oversees national security launches, said in a statement that rocket innovation is "inherently challenging" and that the military will support ULA "in a rigorous, data-driven analysis" of Sunday's incident. The Space Force said in a statement it "secures a variety of providers to ensure no single vehicle transition impacts national security" and expressed confidence that its providers will be able to launch national security missions "in the coming months." By the numbers: Prior to ULA's mishap, Quilty Space , a market research firm, projected a shortfall of around 150 medium- and heavy-lift rocket launches through 2030, according to a report shared with Axios.

 There are 44 medium- and heavy-lift missions facing capacity constraints in 2028, making it the "most acute year of the launch crisis," the report said. Case in point: Commercial satellite companies and startups are now booking rockets that haven't flown yet ; others want to build their own . 

 Reality check: Established rocket makers "typically missed their inaugural launch targets by two to four years," according to Quilty's report.

 New providers average fewer than three launches annually during their first five years, it said. The bottom line: Caleb Henry, Quilty's research director, told me "every failure makes the launch shortage worse," as more critical missions pile up.

 
 
