 3. Toyota&#x27;s space drive 
 
 By Joann Muller and Thomas Novelly 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Toyota, the Japanese industrial giant that evolved from making textile looms to building cars, is exploring another potentially transformative business opportunity: space.

 Why it matters: The booming space industry has a manufacturing problem — and Toyota thinks it can help solve it.

 As demand for satellites soars, Toyota sees an opportunity to apply its expertise in efficient, high-volume manufacturing to an industry that has traditionally relied on expensive, custom-built spacecraft. Driving the news: Toyota revealed its space ambitions in an exclusive interview with Axios, disclosing for the first time that it has invested more than $240 million in 11 space-related startups.

 The investments, through Toyota's three investment arms — Toyota Ventures, Woven Capital and Toyota Invention Partners — span satellite manufacturing, communications, navigation, rocket launches and spacecraft servicing. Between the lines: Toyota's space investments could benefit both its automotive business and the emerging space industry.

 For Toyota, they could mean better satellite connectivity, more precise navigation and new revenue streams beyond automobiles. For space companies, Toyota brings established supply chains and manufacturing expertise that could help them overcome production bottlenecks and lower costs. The space companies Toyota is investing in are:

